  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nPartly cloudy skies and above normal temperatures were observed\nacross the local area this afternoon. Once again, convection is\nexpected to initiate across the Rockies and move into the high\nplains by evening, bringing another (20-30%) chance for\nnocturnal convection across parts of the local area. While not \neveryone will see precipitation overnight, most of the CAMS are \nindicating this high plains convection will reach the local \narea late this evening, weakening as it crosses the region. \nGiven the elevated instability, a few strong to marginally \nsevere wind gusts will again be possible, especially across \nareas mainly west of Highway 183, with 50 to 60 mph wind gusts\nbeing the main concern.\n\nTaking a look at the broader weather pattern, high pressure\nacross the southern plains will transition into the Ohio River\nvalley to start next week, resulting in a southwesterly flow \npattern aloft continuing through much of next week. This \npattern will bring daily weak disturbances crossing the local \narea during the evening/nighttime hours, originating across the \nRockies and tracking eastward each evening. While high pressure \nwill not be directly over the area, heights aloft will remain \nelevated and afternoon temperatures are expected to remain \nseveral degrees (3-8 degrees) above normal through the work \nweek.\n\nLate in the period, both the GFS/EC indicate the upper level\nridge will rebuild westward across the central Plains and \namplify next weekend as an area of low pressure meanders across\nthe Pacific Northwest and a tropical disturbance tracks north \nacross the eastern Pacific. This should diminish storm chances \nnext weekend while allowing temperatures to increase further \nabove climatology...with no significant relief (temperature\nwise) foreseen over at least the next 10 days.",
